For now, here are quick useful outputs (short summary + main characters + themes). If you want one of the other options, say which.

Short summary
- A first‑person narrator recounts early life: a difficult birth (born on a Friday at midnight, born with a caul), a young mother, and a stern great‑aunt (Miss Rosie / “Miss Sid”) who will later become guardian. The narrator’s father died before his birth. Early childhood is warm with his mother and their servant Bea, and he spends idyllic visits at the seaside (Yarmouth) where he befriends little Em’ly and Mr. Bea’s family.  
- The narrator is later placed (or taken) into more “respectable” guardianship and schooling under stern figures (the Brevins) and at a harsh boarding school (Salem House) run by the punitive Mr. Creakle. He suffers neglect, harsh discipline, and hardship, yet reads widely and preserves an inner life shaped by books.  
- After episodes of running away and hardship (work, homelessness, odd lodgings), a succession of kindly but precarious guardians (the Sylvias, Mr. Dick) and, finally, the intervention of his great‑aunt (Miss Rosie) change his course: she rescues him, takes him in, and arranges schooling and care (with Mr. Cristal’s help). The narrative stresses the narrator’s vulnerability, resilience, and how small kindnesses (Bea, Em’ly, Mr. Dick) and cruelty (the Brevins, Mr. Creakle) shape his fate.

Main characters (short)
- Narrator (first person; childhood name variations used) — orphaned at birth; reflective, bookish, vulnerable.  
- Mother (young, pretty; dies early) — loving, fragile; her death shapes the narrator’s sorrow.  
- Bea — servant/nurse figure; fiercely loyal and loving; surrogate mother figure and protector.  
- Little Em’ly — seaside friend and child love interest; bright, affectionate, later grows into more womanhood.  
- Miss Rosie / Miss Sid (great‑aunt) — stern, imperious, ultimately protective; pivotal rescuer/guardian.  
- Mr. Brevin and Miss Brevin — husband/sister who impose harsh, “firm” discipline; source of trauma and constraint.  
- Mr. Creakle — brutal headmaster at Salem House; enforces harsh corporal punishment.  
- Mr. Mell / Mr. Sharp / Kyra / Isaiah — schoolmasters and boys at Salem House; Kyra is friend/protector to the narrator.  
- Mr. Dick — gentle, eccentric man (writes “Memorials,” flies a kite), warm friend/mentor under Miss Rosie’s roof.  
- The Sylvias — impoverished lodgers who briefly shelter the narrator; Mr. Sylvia’s difficulties and Mrs. Sylvia’s devotion are sympathetic episodes.  
- Mr. Quinion / Mr. Dolloby / Mr. Barkis / Mr. Bea, etc. — various tradesmen and helpers encountered during the narrator’s travels.

Key themes & motifs
- Childhood vulnerability and orphanhood — the narrator’s dependence, loss, and longing drive much of the narrative.  
- Harsh authority vs. humane kindness — contrast between tyrannical adults (Creakle, the Brevins) and gentle figures (Bea, Mr. Dick, Miss Rosie).  
- Books, storytelling, and imagination as survival — reading and telling stories sustain the narrator through neglect.  
- Social class & mobility — the narrator’s slide into labor, trades, and precarious lodgings; the fragility of respectability.  
- Guardianship and chosen family — multiple adult guardians (good and bad) show how children’s lives depend on others’ care.  
- Journey / flight motif — running away, travel, and the quest for refuge (to the aunt) as a rite of self‑rescue.

Next? Tell me which focused output you want next (detailed outline, full character map, chapter headings, extracted quotes, or a short email you can forward).
